Hi @user,

Thank you for the review and for catching that crucial edge case.

I hadn't considered the scenario where the original request body is consumed (`originalRequest.bodyUsed` is `true`) but the cloned request for a potential retry is not (`ky.request.bodyUsed` is `false`). My proposed logic with a single combined if condition would indeed fail to clean up the unused cloned stream in that situation, leading to a potential resource leak.

To address this, I agree that we should handle the cleanup for each stream independently. I propose updating the logic as follows:

```ts
.finally(async () => {
    const originalRequest = ky._originalRequest;
    const cleanupPromises = [];

    // Add the original request's stream to the cleanup list if it was never used.
    if (originalRequest && !originalRequest.bodyUsed) {
        cleanupPromises.push(originalRequest.body?.cancel());
    }

    // Add the cloned request's stream to the cleanup list if it was never used.
    if (!ky.request.bodyUsed) {
        cleanupPromises.push(ky.request.body?.cancel());
    }

    // Await all cancellations concurrently.
    if (cleanupPromises.length > 0) {
        await Promise.all(cleanupPromises);
    }
})
```
This approach ensures that:

1. If both streams are unused (the original hang issue), they are both canceled together.
2. If only one of the streams remains unused (the memory leak scenario you pointed out), it is still correctly identified and canceled.

I believe this is a more robust solution that safely covers all cases. What are your thoughts on this updated approach?

I'll push the changes based on your feedback. Thanks again!

